WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.487
The mean and median are both measures of center, but they behave differently when extreme values are present.

00:00:06.487 --> 00:00:08.762
Look at the first set of numbers.

00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:13.202
The values are balanced, so both the mean and median are thirty.

00:00:13.202 --> 00:00:16.597
Now replace the largest value with four hundred.

00:00:16.597 --> 00:00:20.867
The median stays at thirty, but the mean increases dramatically.

00:00:21.409 --> 00:00:23.561
This does not mean the mean is bad.

00:00:23.561 --> 00:00:31.616
It means the mean answers a different question and is sensitive to every value.

00:00:31.616 --> 00:00:38.731
For variables such as income, home prices, hospital charges, or transaction amounts, distributions are often skewed.

00:00:38.731 --> 00:00:44.286
In those cases, the median may better represent a typical observation.

00:00:44.286 --> 00:00:51.356
A useful EDA habit is to compare mean and median instead of reporting only one of them.

00:00:51.356 --> 00:00:57.159
If they are very different, investigate the distribution and possible extreme values.
